{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_bsnh7jzovqn52v62jesytp36tpnxlmrqypexzv35d2leilvxjlcq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_0195CJeVKFmy3PefVsRLb6Tg","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-aPmyYd/translated_rust/SYMBOLS.md","old_string":"The Rust `.so` imports `printf@GLIBC_2.2.5` (the translation deliberately calls\nlibc `printf` rather than Rust `std::io` so the emitted bytes *and* the stdout\nbuffering behaviour match). It does **not** import `strchr` because `strchr` is\nre-implemented byte-for-byte in `src/driver.rs`; this is an implementation\ndetail that is invisible across the ABI and is covered by the differential\ntests. Its remaining imports (`_Unwind_*`, `malloc`, `memcpy`, `dl_iterate_phdr`,\n…) are libc / libgcc_s runtime support pulled in by Rust `std`.","new_string":"The Rust `.so` imports **the same two**: `printf@GLIBC_2.2.5` and\n`strchr@GLIBC_2.2.5`. Both are deliberately taken from libc rather than\nreimplemented, so that the emitted bytes, the stdout buffering behaviour and the\nfaulting behaviour on out-of-bounds/NULL pointers are identical to the C\nlibrary's (see the `strchr` note in `src/driver.rs`; the original translation\nhand-rolled `strchr` and that caused a real divergence — see \"Divergence found\nand fixed\" below). Its remaining imports (`_Unwind_*`, `malloc`, `memcpy`,\n`dl_iterate_phdr`, …) are libc / libgcc_s runtime support pulled in by Rust\n`std`.\n\n## Divergence found and fixed\n\nThe hand-written `strchr` in the original translation dereferenced the raw\npointer with `*p`. In a `dev` build rustc's debug assertions insert a\nnull-pointer-dereference check there, which **panics**; the panic then crosses\nthe `extern \"C\"` boundary of `foo`, which is a non-unwinding boundary, so the\nprocess **aborts**. Result:\n\n| input | C `.so` | Rust `.so` (before) | Rust `.so` (after) |\n|-------|---------|---------------------|--------------------|\n| `foo(NULL, 'A')` | killed by `SIGSEGV` (11) | killed by `SIGABRT` (6) | killed by `SIGSEGV` (11) |\n| `foo(NULL, '\\0')` | `SIGSEGV` | `SIGABRT` | `SIGSEGV` |\n| `driver(NULL)`   | `SIGSEGV` | `SIGABRT` | `SIGSEGV` |\n\nFixed by calling libc `strchr` — the very function `driver.c` calls — with the\n`char`→`int` integer promotion spelled out at the call site (`c as c_int`).\n\n## Test-harness hazard worth recording\n\n`cargo test` does **not** rebuild a `cdylib`-only lib target, because\nintegration tests never link against it. The suite therefore loaded whatever\n`target/<profile>/libdriver.so` an earlier `cargo build` had left behind, and\nsource edits appeared to have no effect. `tests/differential.rs` now asserts\nthat the `.so` is newer than everything in `src/` and than `Cargo.toml`, and\n`run_tests.sh` always runs `cargo build` before `cargo test`."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":846,"cache_read_input_tokens":102945,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":846,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a2685c3d-ee79-4864-97fb-3b0c1854d5cb","uuid":"f3f10087-2945-43bd-b23f-0c255e35dc1f","timestamp":"2026-08-18T06:41:53.033Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_0195CJeVKFmy3PefVsRLb6Tg","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-aPmyYd/translated_rust/SYMBOLS.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a2685c3d-ee79-4864-97fb-3b0c1854d5cb","uuid":"e7ebb1e6-e336-4047-9eb0-79ff25db322b","timestamp":"2026-08-18T06:41:53.041Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-aPmyYd/translated_rust/SYMBOLS.md","oldString":"The Rust `.so` imports `printf@GLIBC_2.2.5` (the translation deliberately calls\nlibc `printf` rather than Rust `std::io` so the emitted bytes *and* the stdout\nbuffering behaviour match). It does **not** import `strchr` because `strchr` is\nre-implemented byte-for-byte in `src/driver.rs`; this is an implementation\ndetail that is invisible across the ABI and is covered by the differential\ntests. Its remaining imports (`_Unwind_*`, `malloc`, `memcpy`, `dl_iterate_phdr`,\n…) are libc / libgcc_s runtime support pulled in by Rust `std`.","newString":"The Rust `.so` imports **the same two**: `printf@GLIBC_2.2.5` and\n`strchr@GLIBC_2.2.5`. Both are deliberately taken from libc rather than\nreimplemented, so that the emitted bytes, the stdout buffering behaviour and the\nfaulting behaviour on out-of-bounds/NULL pointers are identical to the C\nlibrary's (see the `strchr` note in `src/driver.rs`; the original translation\nhand-rolled `strchr` and that caused a real divergence — see \"Divergence found\nand fixed\" below). Its remaining imports (`_Unwind_*`, `malloc`, `memcpy`,\n`dl_iterate_phdr`, …) are libc / libgcc_s runtime support pulled in by Rust\n`std`.\n\n## Divergence found and fixed\n\nThe hand-written `strchr` in the original translation dereferenced the raw\npointer with `*p`. In a `dev` build rustc's debug assertions insert a\nnull-pointer-dereference check there, which **panics**; the panic then crosses\nthe `extern \"C\"` boundary of `foo`, which is a non-unwinding boundary, so the\nprocess **aborts**. Result:\n\n| input | C `.so` | Rust `.so` (before) | Rust `.so` (after) |\n|-------|---------|---------------------|--------------------|\n| `foo(NULL, 'A')` | killed by `SIGSEGV` (11) | killed by `SIGABRT` (6) | killed by `SIGSEGV` (11) |\n| `foo(NULL, '\\0')` | `SIGSEGV` | `SIGABRT` | `SIGSEGV` |\n| `driver(NULL)`   | `SIGSEGV` | `SIGABRT` | `SIGSEGV` |\n\nFixed by calling libc `strchr` — the very function `driver.c` calls — with the\n`char`→`int` integer promotion spelled out at the call site (`c as c_int`).\n\n## Test-harness hazard worth recording\n\n`cargo test` does **not** rebuild a `cdylib`-only lib target, because\nintegration tests never link against it. The suite therefore loaded whatever\n`target/<profile>/libdriver.so` an earlier `cargo build` had left behind, and\nsource edits appeared to have no effect. `tests/differential.rs` now asserts\nthat the `.so` is newer than everything in `src/` and than `Cargo.toml`, and\n`run_tests.sh` always runs `cargo build` before `cargo test`.","originalFile":"# SYMBOLS.md — Phase A: exported-symbol surface\n\nDerived mechanically:\n\n```\ncd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build \\\n  && cmake .. -DCMAKE_POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE=ON && cmake --build .\nnm -D --defined-only c_src/build/libdriver.so\nnm -D --defined-only target/debug/libdriver.so\n```\n\n## C source inventory (whole library — nothing is excluded)\n\nThe CMake target `driver` compiles exactly one translation unit:\n\n| C file | lines | functions with external linkage |\n|--------|-------|---------------------------------|\n| `c_src/src/driver.c`     | 40 | `foo`, `driver` |\n| `c_src/include/driver.h` | 28 | (declares `driver` only; header guard `DRIVER_H_` is the only preprocessor construct) |\n\nThere are **no** namespace/renaming macros in the header, so the linker names\nare exactly the source-level names. `foo` is *not* `static`, so it has external\nlinkage and is exported even though it is absent from the public header.\n\n## Defined dynamic symbols\n\n| # | symbol | C `.so` | Rust `.so` | C prototype | Rust definition |\n|---|--------|---------|------------|-------------|-----------------|\n| 1 | `driver` | `T` (0x1176) | `T` | `void driver(const char *in)`     | `src/driver.rs` `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern \"C\" fn driver` |\n| 2 | `foo`    | `T` (0x1129) | `T` | `int  foo(const char *in, char c)` | `src/driver.rs` `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern \"C\" fn foo` |\n\n**Symbol diff (C exports − Rust exports) = EMPTY.** Verified automatically by\nthe test `phase_d_symbol_parity` in `tests/differential.rs`, which reruns `nm -D`\non both shared objects and asserts set equality, so the gate cannot silently rot.\n\nNothing was missing, so no `#[no_mangle]` wrapper had to be added and no\nuntranslated C module had to be back-filled: `driver.c` is the entire library\nand both of its external-linkage functions were already translated.\n\n## Undefined (imported) symbols\n\nThe C `.so` imports `printf@GLIBC_2.2.5` and `strchr@GLIBC_2.2.5` (plus the\nusual weak `_ITM_*` / `__gmon_start__` / `__cxa_finalize`).\n\nThe Rust `.so` imports `printf@GLIBC_2.2.5` (the translation deliberately calls\nlibc `printf` rather than Rust `std::io` so the emitted bytes *and* the stdout\nbuffering behaviour match). It does **not** import `strchr` because `strchr` is\nre-implemented byte-for-byte in `src/driver.rs`; this is an implementation\ndetail that is invisible across the ABI and is covered by the differential\ntests. Its remaining imports (`_Unwind_*`, `malloc`, `memcpy`, `dl_iterate_phdr`,\n…) are libc / libgcc_s runtime support pulled in by Rust `std`.\n\n**0 missing symbols and 0 undefined non-libc symbols in the Rust `.so`.**\nVerified with:\n\n```\nldd -r target/debug/libdriver.so     # no \"undefined symbol\" lines\n```\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":42,"oldLines":14,"newStart":42,"newLines":42,"lines":[" The C `.so` imports `printf@GLIBC_2.2.5` and `strchr@GLIBC_2.2.5` (plus the"," usual weak `_ITM_*` / `__gmon_start__` / `__cxa_finalize`)."," ","-The Rust `.so` imports `printf@GLIBC_2.2.5` (the translation deliberately calls","-libc `printf` rather than Rust `std::io` so the emitted bytes *and* the stdout","-buffering behaviour match). It does **not** import `strchr` because `strchr` is","-re-implemented byte-for-byte in `src/driver.rs`; this is an implementation","-detail that is invisible across the ABI and is covered by the differential","-tests. Its remaining imports (`_Unwind_*`, `malloc`, `memcpy`, `dl_iterate_phdr`,","-…) are libc / libgcc_s runtime support pulled in by Rust `std`.","+The Rust `.so` imports **the same two**: `printf@GLIBC_2.2.5` and","+`strchr@GLIBC_2.2.5`. Both are deliberately taken from libc rather than","+reimplemented, so that the emitted bytes, the stdout buffering behaviour and the","+faulting behaviour on out-of-bounds/NULL pointers are identical to the C","+library's (see the `strchr` note in `src/driver.rs`; the original translation","+hand-rolled `strchr` and that caused a real divergence — see \"Divergence found","+and fixed\" below). Its remaining imports (`_Unwind_*`, `malloc`, `memcpy`,","+`dl_iterate_phdr`, …) are libc / libgcc_s runtime support pulled in by Rust","+`std`."," ","+## Divergence found and fixed","+","+The hand-written `strchr` in the original translation dereferenced the raw","+pointer with `*p`. In a `dev` build rustc's debug assertions insert a","+null-pointer-dereference check there, which **panics**; the panic then crosses","+the `extern \"C\"` boundary of `foo`, which is a non-unwinding boundary, so the","+process **aborts**. Result:","+","+| input | C `.so` | Rust `.so` (before) | Rust `.so` (after) |","+|-------|---------|---------------------|--------------------|","+| `foo(NULL, 'A')` | killed by `SIGSEGV` (11) | killed by `SIGABRT` (6) | killed by `SIGSEGV` (11) |","+| `foo(NULL, '\\0')` | `SIGSEGV` | `SIGABRT` | `SIGSEGV` |","+| `driver(NULL)`   | `SIGSEGV` | `SIGABRT` | `SIGSEGV` |","+","+Fixed by calling libc `strchr` — the very function `driver.c` calls — with the","+`char`→`int` integer promotion spelled out at the call site (`c as c_int`).","+","+## Test-harness hazard worth recording","+","+`cargo test` does **not** rebuild a `cdylib`-only lib target, because","+integration tests never link against it. The suite therefore loaded whatever","+`target/<profile>/libdriver.so` an earlier `cargo build` had left behind, and","+source edits appeared to have no effect. `tests/differential.rs` now asserts","+that the `.so` is newer than everything in `src/` and than `Cargo.toml`, and","+`run_tests.sh` always runs `cargo build` before `cargo test`.","+"," **0 missing symbols and 0 undefined non-libc symbols in the Rust `.so`.**"," Verified with:"," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
