{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_yiutsgkvrsc45mpsgbqjacu4h32n4ar7nxpxa257krnxwcw6erzq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. Summary:\n\n## Result: one real bug found and fixed\n\n**The divergence:** the C `foo` calls glibc `strchr`; the Rust translation hand-rolled its own `strchr` with `*p`. In a `dev` build rustc's debug assertions insert a null-pointer-dereference check there, which **panics**, and the panic crossing `foo`'s non-unwinding `extern \"C\"` boundary **aborts**:\n\n| input | C `.so` | Rust `.so` (before) | Rust `.so` (after) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `foo(NULL, 'A')` | `SIGSEGV` (11) | `SIGABRT` (6) | `SIGSEGV` (11) |\n| `foo(NULL, '\\0')` | `SIGSEGV` | `SIGABRT` | `SIGSEGV` |\n| `driver(NULL)` | `SIGSEGV` | `SIGABRT` | `SIGSEGV` |\n\nFixed in `src/driver.rs` by calling libc `strchr` — the same function `driver.c` calls — with the `char`→`int` promotion spelled out (`c as c_int`). The Rust `.so` now imports the identical libc set (`printf`, `strchr`).\n\n**A harness hazard worth flagging:** `cargo test` **never rebuilds a `cdylib`-only lib target**, so the suite silently tested a stale `libdriver.so` and my first fix appeared to do nothing. `tests/differential.rs` now asserts the `.so` is newer than `src/` and `Cargo.toml`, and `run_tests.sh` always builds first.\n\n## Phases\n- **A** — `SYMBOLS.md` (2 symbols: `foo`, `driver`; `foo` is exported despite being absent from the header), `ERRORS.md` (13 rows), `CONFIGS.md` (27 rows). Grepping the C proved it has *zero* explicit error returns, asserts, range checks or `#ifdef` axes — its whole error surface is implicit faults, so the faulting rows are verified by comparing the **termination signal** of re-exec'd children, using `mmap` guard pages to make out-of-bounds scans fault deterministically.\n- **B** — all 27 rows pass over randomized inputs (fixed seed), driving the low-level `foo` directly, not just the `driver` wrapper.\n- **C** — all 13 rows pass, including NULL pointers, zero/oversized lengths, unterminated buffers, `c == '\\0'` (which makes the C loop non-terminating), and out-of-range `int` values in the `char` slot — deliberately including `0x100` and `i32::MIN`, whose low byte is `0x00` and which are therefore *fatal* in both implementations.\n- **D** — symbol diff **empty** in both `dev` and `release`; no unresolved symbols. Only one feature combination exists (no `[features]` table), but `run_tests.sh` derives the power set mechanically and runs both profiles: **34/34 tests pass in each**.\n\nI also mutation-tested the suite (broken `s++`, broken `res++`, wrong needle, wrong format literal, and the original `strchr` bug) — all caught. One mutation (`c as u8 as c_int`) is *not* caught, correctly so: `strchr` compares `(unsigned char)c`, so sign- vs zero-extension of the low byte is unobservable, and all 255 non-zero needle values are exercised anyway. Nothing in `c_src/` was modified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":458,"cache_read_input_tokens":112910,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":458,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a2685c3d-ee79-4864-97fb-3b0c1854d5cb","uuid":"91245014-62fd-413c-95e0-e34be01ccbe9","timestamp":"2026-08-18T06:43:27.369Z"}
